# Comments in JavaScript

We just said that the JavaScript engine runs our code from top to bottom, line by line. **Comments** are one exception to that. A comment is text in your file that the engine does not interpret at all, and there are two reasons to write one: to add a natural-language explanation of what the code does, and to specify that a certain part of the code should not run.

Just like the HTML comments we saw in Module 1, JavaScript has two kinds. The syntax is different, but the idea is the same.

## Single-Line Comments

A single-line comment starts with two forward slashes, `//`. Everything after those slashes, to the end of that line, is a comment.

Run this cell:

```{javascript autorun}
console.log('line 1');
// console.log('line 2');
console.log('line 3');
```

`line 2` never appears, because that line is commented out. Most code editors color comments differently, usually green or gray, so you can see at a glance which lines are inert. Try removing the `//` and running the cell again, then putting it back.

A comment does not have to take up the whole line. Put `//` after some code and the code still runs; only the rest of the line is ignored:

```{javascript autorun}
console.log(3 + 3); // logs the value 6 to the console
```

That is the other use of comments: describing in plain English what a line is doing, for yourself in six months or for whoever reads your code next.

## Block Comments

The other kind is a **block comment**, sometimes called a multi-line comment. It starts with `/*` and ends with `*/`. Everything between those two markers is a comment and is not interpreted by the engine.

The two markers can sit on the same line or on different lines, so a block comment can cover as much code as you like:

```{javascript autorun}
console.log('line 1');
/*
console.log('line 2');
console.log('line 3');
*/
console.log('line 4');
```

Only `line 1` and `line 4` are logged. Move the `*/` up one line and run it again to bring `line 2` back.

Block comments are also how you disable a whole region at once. Put `/*` above the first line you want to switch off and `*/` below the last one, and everything in between stops running.

### Comments in the Middle of a Line

Because a block comment ends wherever you put `*/`, it can also sit *inside* a line of code. This is a small trick that is very useful while you are experimenting:

```{javascript autorun}
console.log(3 /* + 4 */);
```

That logs `3`. The `+ 4` is still sitting there in the file, so you can bring it back by deleting the two markers, but as far as the engine is concerned the line reads `console.log(3);`. Try deleting the `/*` and `*/` and running the cell again.

## Comments in VS Code

You will not usually type these characters by hand. Most code editors have a keyboard shortcut for toggling comments, and VS Code is no exception: put the cursor on a line and press `Ctrl + /` (Windows) or `Cmd + /` (Mac). If the line is not commented, it gets commented out. Press it again and the comment is removed.

The same shortcut works on a selection. Select several lines, press `Ctrl + /` or `Cmd + /`, and the whole region is commented out; press it again with the region still selected and it comes back. When you are hunting for which line broke your page, this is the fastest tool you have.

## Exercises

1. Comment out the middle line so that the cell logs only `first` and `third`.

    ```{javascript}
    console.log('first');
    console.log('second');
    console.log('third');
    ```
    ```+solution
    console.log('first');
    // console.log('second');
    console.log('third');
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(console.history().map(entry => entry.text).join(',')).should('equal', 'first,third').run('The cell should log only `first` and `third`', 'Commented out the middle line');
    wrap(source.includes('second')).should('equal', true).run('Comment the line out rather than deleting it', 'The line is still there, commented out');
    ```

2. Using a block comment, disable the `+ 4` part of the line below without deleting it, so that the cell logs `3`.

    ```{javascript}
    console.log(3 + 4);
    ```
    ```+solution
    console.log(3 /* + 4 */);
    ```
    ```+test
    check.consoleLogged(/^3$/).run('The cell should log `3`', 'Logged 3');
    wrap(source.includes('4')).should('equal', true).run('Comment the `+ 4` out with `/*` and `*/` rather than deleting it', 'The `+ 4` is still there, inside a block comment');
    ```

3. Work out what this script logs once the engine skips the comments.

    ```{mcq}
    What does this script log?

    ~~~javascript
    console.log('a');
    // console.log('b');
    console.log('c'); // console.log('d');
    /*
    console.log('e');
    */
    console.log('f');
    ~~~

    correct: Correct! `b` and `e` are commented out, and `d` is too, because a `//` hides everything from there to the end of that line.
    wrong: Take the lines one at a time. A `//` hides the rest of its line, and a `/*` hides everything up to the next `*/`, however many lines that covers.
    [x] `a`, `c`, `f`
      correct: Right: the only lines the engine runs are the three that are not inside a comment.
      wrong: This is the one. `b` is behind a `//`, `d` is behind a `//` in the middle of a line, and `e` is inside the block comment.
    [ ] `a`, `c`, `d`, `f`
      wrong: Look again at the line with `c`. The `//` hides everything after it to the end of that line, and `console.log('d');` is after it.
    [ ] `a`, `c`, `e`, `f`
      wrong: A block comment covers everything between `/*` and `*/`, and `console.log('e');` sits between them, so it never runs.
    [ ] `a`, `b`, `c`, `d`, `e`, `f`
      wrong: The engine does not run commented-out code. Three of these six lines are inside comments.
    ```

